   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "You can&#39;t have multi_normal_cholesky on the right of an assignment arrow, it needs to be a tilde:<div><font size=\"2\"><span style=\"background-color:rgba(255,255,255,0)\">g ~<span></span> multi_normal_cholesky(mu, L) ;</span></font></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div><br><br>On Friday, 27 May 2016, Kayhan Batmanghelich &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">batman...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:<br></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><br><div><p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">Dear Stan users,</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">This is my first try with Stan (pyStan). First, I am trying to simulate data from a simple gaussian process. I followed the code from page 162 of the documentation but I run into this error and I cannot figure out what is wrong here:</p>\r\n<p style=\"margin:1.2em 0px!important\">Am I missing something?</p>\r\n<pre style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;font-size:1em;line-height:1.2em;margin:1.2em 0px\"><code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(234,234,234);border-radius:3px;display:inline;background-color:rgb(248,248,248);white-space:pre-wrap;overflow:auto;border-radius:3px;border:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding:0.5em 0.7em;display:block!important\">test_code =&quot;&quot;&quot;\r\ndata {\r\n   int&lt;lower=1&gt; N;  \r\n   cov_matrix[N] K; \r\n}\r\n\r\ntransformed data {\r\n    cholesky_factor_corr[N] L;\r\n    vector[N]  mu;\r\n\r\n    for (j in 1:N) {\r\n     mu[j] &lt;- 0;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    L &lt;- cholesky_decompose(K) ;\r\n}\r\n\r\nparameters {\r\n\r\n   vector[N]   y;\r\n\r\n}\r\n\r\n\r\nmodel {\r\n   vector[N]  g;\r\n\r\n   // data generation\r\n   g &lt;- multi_normal_cholesky(mu, L) ;\r\n\r\n   y ~ normal(mu + g, 1 ) ;\r\n }\r\n&quot;&quot;&quot;\r\n\r\nK = np.random.rand(50,10000)/100\r\nK = K.dot(K.T)\r\n\r\n#print K\r\n\r\ntest_dat = {&#39;N&#39;: 50,\r\n            &#39;K&#39;: K }\r\n\r\nsimTest = pystan.stan(model_code=test_code, data=test_dat,\r\n                  iter=1000, chains=1)</code><code style=\"font-size:0.85em;font-family:Consolas,Inconsolata,Courier,monospace;margin:0px 0.15em;padding:0px 0.3em;white-space:pre-wrap;border:1px solid rgb(2